  "$comment": "Two ways of asking how concentrated Alberta's economy is. effectiveIndustries is 1 divided by the Herfindahl-Hirschman index of industry shares: the number of equally sized industries that would give the same concentration. Higher means more spread out. The jobs measure uses 19 non-overlapping LFS industries; the GDP measure uses the 20 two-digit NAICS sectors at current prices. Aggregates such as \"Energy sector\" and \"Goods-producing industries\" are deliberately excluded from both, because they overlap the sectors and would double-count.",
  "source": "Statistics Canada tables 36-10-0711-01 (GDP by industry, provinces) and 14-10-0023-01 (Labour force characteristics by industry, annual)",
  "method": "For each year: share_i = industry_i / total; HHI = sum of share_i squared; effectiveIndustries = 1 / HHI. Shares use current dollars for GDP because chained-dollar components are not additive and would not sum to the total.",
